  "strategy": "<h1> Approach and Rationale </h1>\nWe propose a two-phase optimization strategy with an initial population-based optimization followed by an adaptive local search using projected gradients and coordinate descent. Unlike standard Frank-Wolfe or projected gradient methods that target the maximum element, this approach minimizes the ratio of the maximum auto-convolution coefficient to the square of the sum of the sequence. \n\nThe key idea is to use hyperparameter-tuned distributions:\n- Multi-start initialization: Gaussian and Bell-shaped sequences of variable lengths (256, 512, 768) with optimization-resistant smoothing.\n- Local refinement: Mirror descent with tempered entropy to optimize the sequence structure.\n\n<h2> Mathematical Specification </h2>\nWe aim to minimize the objective:  \n2 * n * max(convolve(a,a)) / (sum(a))\u00b2  \nsubject to:  \n- a\u1d62 \u2265 0\n- sum(a) \u2265 0.01\n- max(a\u1d62) < 1000\n\nThe optimization uses coordinate-wise projections to maintain:\n- The sum constraint: normalization via simplex projection\n- Non-negativity: density via coordinate-wise thresholding\n\n<h2> Implementation Procedure </h2>\nWe use three distinct optimization phases:\n\n<3> Phase 1: Population-based Initialization\n- Generate candidates of lengths 256 (sigma=0.4), 512 (sigma=0.6), 768 (sigma=0.8) with Gaussian and Bell shapes\n- Each Gaussian distribution uses:  \n  a\u1d62 \u221d exp(-( (i-position)/i_range )\u00b2 / \u03c3\u00b2 )\n- Bell distribution:  \n  a\u1d62 = (max(0, 1 - | shifted_i |\u2074 )) rescaled to probability density\n\n<4> Phase 2: Hyperparameter Tuning with Robust Resets\n- Track best candidate from short and long sequences\n- Reset optimization with Gaussian noise perturbation if convergence failure is detected\n- Parameters to monitor:  \n  - Convergence failure threshold (\u03c4_converge = 12)\n  - Simplify if elementary peak reduction cannot be convinced\n\n<5> Phase 3: Probabilistic Auto-convolution Optimization with Progressive Analysis\n- Steepest descent in probability space:  \n  f \u2190 f / (sum(f))       [Normalize candidate]\n  conv = fast_convolve(f) [Linear convolution using FFT]\n  score = 2 * len(f) * max(conv) / (sum(f))\u00b2\n\n- Gradient estimation: Compute top peak contributions, then use average subgradient across all active constraints\n- Stops automatically when:  \n  - Objective stabilizes (variation < 0.01% over 50 steps)\n  - Alternative representation becomes more promising\n\n<6> Key helper functions:\n- `fast_convolve`: FFT-based linear convolution with approximate scaling\n- `project_simplex`: Euclidean projection to probability simplex\n- `evaluate_sequence`: Provided function call for validation\n\n<h2> Search Choices and Variations </h2>\nThe algorithm uses a fixed-radius spiral search around the best candidate from prior optimization runs:\n1. Radius starts at 0.1/len(seq) for each sequence starting point\n2. Update step: radius * 2\u03b2 for \u03b2 moving toward its stability value\n3. Candidate generation considers up to 4 different starting shapes: uniform, Gaussian, Bell, asymmetric\n4. Automatic pause at 10% or min(300, seq_sumexec_time) of function evaluation time\n5. Resampling with interpolation at specified intervals\n\n<h2> Time Budget and Validation </h2>\n- Total runtime cap: 1800 seconds\n- Active monitoring: \n  <ul>\n  <li>Wall-clock time when score stabilizes (no improvement \u2265 25 standard deviations)</li>\n  <li>Thread local storage of top candidate found</li>\n  </ul>\n\nWe implement fallback protection by:\n- Backing up best candidate before any destructive operation\n- If evaluation fails, revert to previous best candidate\n- For each sequence starting point, keep at least 3 versions\n\n<h2> Complexity and Hardware Use </h2>\nThis approach balances:\n- FFT convolution cost proportional to O(n log n) for each evaluation\n- Simplex projection O(n) per candidate\n- Maximum-length auto-convolution O(n), manageable with heap-spacing\n\n<h2> Output Function </h2>\nA helper computes minimal boundary step sizes and ensures:\n1. The objective function is minimized below 1.5\n2. The evaluation sequence meets the input isLoggedIn Fibonacci sequence constraints\n\n<h1> End of Detailed Implementation Plan </h1>",
